[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Spice V2 - Clean C++20 Architecture and Interface Contracts

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Version 2.0, 2026-11-20. Supersedes v1 Rust prototype architecture for the V2 reset while preserving core ``ARCH-*`` UIDs and clean numerical boundaries.

Spice V2 is a standalone, modern C++20 analog, RF, and mixed-signal circuit simulator designed to replace ngspice without inheriting SPICE3's global mutable state, C macro preprocessor, or interactive Nutmeg control shell. It adopts a Xyce-inspired universal DAE architecture without Trilinos or MPI build overhead, using SuiteSparse KLU (LGPL-2.1+ shared library) for sparse circuit factorization and a single generic OSDI 0.3/0.4 loader for OpenVAF-compiled Verilog-A compact models.

To deliver immediate real-circuit utility and a continuous differential oracle, V2 uses a Strangler Fig transition: a thin external-process XyceBackend executes the Canonical IR on Day 1 (fully GPL-isolated), and NativeCppBackend (C++20 + KLU + OSDI) replaces it slice-by-slice behind the exact same ISimulationBackend and Canonical IR contracts.

Core engine licensing targets MIT or Apache-2.0, allowing LGPL-2.1+ dynamic libraries (SuiteSparse KLU). Authoring and automation are first-class in C#/.NET (including StarLark-cs and SharpUnits) and F#, plus a schematic capture UI sharing a single fail-closed Unified Device Descriptor.

Statuses are honest: Accepted records a settled architecture decision; Proposed/Draft records unimplemented requirements; Implemented/Verified requires executable evidence links.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: 1. Architecture goals and fixed boundaries

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ARCH-GOAL-001
STATUS: Accepted
TITLE: Independent C++20 core
STATEMENT: >>>
The simulator shall use a self-contained C++20 numerical and circuit core with no compile-time or link-time dependency on ngspice, Xyce, Trilinos, a graphical interface, or an interactive command shell.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: Build and test the headless C++20 core library and C ABI without GUI, shell, or legacy simulator libraries; inspect CMake targets and linked libraries.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ARCH-GOAL-002
STATUS: Accepted
TITLE: Replaceable numerical backends
STATEMENT: >>>
The simulator shall expose circuit-independent numerical contracts through which qualified CPU and optional future GPU solver libraries can be exchanged without modifying device equations or public request schemas.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: Run the same qualified circuit and matrix cases through SuiteSparse KLU and an independently implemented dense reference solver and compare outcomes and residuals.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ARCH-GOAL-003
STATUS: Accepted
TITLE: Independent model ecosystem
STATEMENT: >>>
The simulator shall support built-in C++20 models and versioned compiled-model adapters (OSDI 0.3/0.4) without allowing model-specific layouts, dialect syntax, or native plugin types into the numerical core.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: Load an OpenVAF-compiled OSDI model through the generic adapter and inspect core interfaces for forbidden vendor/plugin types.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ARCH-GOAL-004
STATUS: Accepted
TITLE: Automation-first and schematic-ready interfaces
STATEMENT: >>>
The simulator shall expose typed Canonical IR inputs, job states, signals, measurements, diagnostics, and results to C++, CLI, C#, F#, StarLark-cs, and schematic UI clients without requiring console-output parsing.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: Execute an end-to-end circuit from CLI, C#, StarLark-cs, and schematic IR emission and recover typed results and structured diagnostics.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ARCH-GOAL-005
STATUS: Accepted
TITLE: Qualified behavior and honest outcomes
STATEMENT: >>>
The simulator shall distinguish numerical correctness, model validity, execution completeness, and assertion outcomes and shall associate capability claims with reproducible verification evidence.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: Inspect test evidence for analytic, nonconvergent, cancelled, out-of-domain, and completed-but-failing cases.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: 2. Component layout and dependency direction

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
V2 layout under v2/:
- include/spice/capi.h: stable flat extern "C" ABI (opaque handles, version checks, contiguous buffers).
- cpp/core: typed IDs, Canonical IR, Unified Device Descriptor registry, diagnostics, CSC pattern contracts.
- cpp/dae: universal Device interface (F, Q, dF/dx, dQ/dx, noise PSDs, trial/commit rollback).
- cpp/devices: built-in passives, transmission lines, sources, controlled sources, compiled B-source autodiff VM, switches.
- cpp/osdi: generic OSDI 0.3/0.4 dynamic model loader.
- cpp/linalg: CSC matrix assembly, SuiteSparse KLU shared-library adapter (real/complex), dense reference solver.
- cpp/solver: dimensioned Newton, voltage limiting, source/Gmin continuation with mandatory physical final solve.
- cpp/analysis: OP, DC, AC, TRAN, Noise, PZ, TF, Sens, SP, STB, FFT, HB, PSS, PAC, PNoise, QPSS.
- cpp/mixed: separate event-driven Verilog-AMS digital/wreal co-simulation kernel (M4).
- cpp/parser: canonical SPICE3f5 parser/elaborator, dialect profiles, SPEF and fail-closed Spectre subset.
- cpp/audit: opt-in independent physical consistency audits.
- cpp/capi: thin extern "C" wrapper with exception containment.
- dotnet/: Spice.Ir, Spice.Sdk (ISimulationBackend, NativeCppBackend, XyceBackend), Spice.Starlark, Spice.Schematic, Spice.App.

Dependency spine: capi -> analysis -> solver -> linalg -> core; analysis -> dae -> core; devices/osdi -> dae. No reverse edge from linalg, dae, or devices to parsers, C ABI, or .NET SDK.
<<<

